#!/usr/bin/python3
"""sfduo-system-screen: the page left of the left panel (#109).

    sfduo-system-screen          run, on the session bus as org.sfduo.SystemScreen
    sfduo-system-screen --demo   open and close it N times (SFDUO_DEMO, 10),
                                 logging each animation's frames

The Surface Duo 2 has a page left of its home screens, reached by a swipe from
the desktop; this is the port's. It takes the left panel, slides in from the
left edge, and its background goes from the desktop's black to a soft light
tone as it comes, the content appearing towards the end - the user is there
briefly, and the light breaks the black.

The colour change is a new frame on every step, which GTK3 - the dock -
draws on the CPU here: a panel repainted each frame once held the grid to
8 fps. GTK4 draws through the GPU here, so this is a program of its own: the
background is one colour node per frame, the content's fade an opacity node,
the slide the surface's margin. What it costs is measured, not assumed: each
animation leaves a line in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/sfduo-system-screen.perf.

Driven by a progress 0..1 on the session bus - SetProgress(d), Open(), Close()
- so that the dock's swipe can hold it under the finger (#111). Closed, the
surface is unmapped and draws nothing.
"""
